# Removing update-manager and ubuntu-pro-client takes Ubuntu's metapackages,
# which depend on them, with them.
Conflicts: apport-gtk, hwctl, ubuntu-advantage-desktop-daemon, ubuntu-insights,
           ubuntu-pro-client, ubuntu-report, update-manager, update-notifier,
           whoopsie
Provides: update-notifier
# The taskbar's ID is patrone-taskbar@patroneos.com from 0.4.8, and the
# session mode (session/make-session-mode) names it
Breaks: patrone-gnome-taskbar (<< 0.4.8)
Description: PatroneOS's desktop
 Ubuntu's desktop with Patrone's branding and apps, and none of Canonical's
 own services: no Ubuntu Pro (its client, its services and its notices), no
 Software Updater (Patrone Software tells of updates and installs them), no
 hwctl (Canonical's hardware-certification tool), and nothing that reports
 to Canonical: no whoopsie (error reports), ubuntu-insights or ubuntu-report
 (system reports), and no apport-gtk, the crash dialog that offered to send
 them. Crash reports still collect in /var/crash for debugging, and nothing
 sends them. First-login setup skips Ubuntu's page that asks to send reports
 and its Ubuntu Pro page. It lists Ubuntu's desktop packages itself, in
 place of Ubuntu's metapackages (ubuntu-desktop-minimal and ubuntu-minimal),
 which need Ubuntu Pro and Software Updater.
 .
 Patrone's own updates come from its apt repository on GitHub Pages
 (billsecond.github.io/patrone-apt), whose key and source it installs; its
 builds of Ubuntu's GDM, GNOME Settings and first-login setup come before
 Ubuntu's own. The clock is set from NTS servers run by Cloudflare, Netnod
 and PTB, NetworkManager checks for Wi-Fi sign-in pages with GNOME's server,
 and nothing offers to upgrade to Ubuntu's next release. Its app store is GNOME
 Software, with Patrone's picks, in place of Canonical's App Center. What
 the system connects to, and who runs each service, is in
 /usr/share/doc/patrone-desktop/connections.txt.
 .
 It also brings a few everyday tools: fastfetch (system information, in
 Patrone's colors), nano and the full vim; btop and htop (what's running);
 tree, ripgrep (rg) and fzf (finding files and text); bat (files with
 highlighting, run as batcat); ncdu (what's using the disk); and 7zip (7z).
